I can spot exactly why your data is just spinning and not loading! There are two issues causing this: a typo in your API path and a missing useEffect to trigger the API call when the screen opens.
Here is the precise fix for both.
### 1. Fix the API Endpoint Typo
In your code, the API path has some stray question marks inside the string ("/JS/ ????voucher/..."), which will cause a 404 error. Based on your backend VoucherRequestServiceImpl.java code, the correct endpoint that returns the formatted stepper statuses is getAllRequestsSummary().
**FIND this line inside your fetchMyBatches function:**
```javascript
const data = await callApi("/JS/ ????voucher/all-requests-summary", null, "GET");

```
**REPLACE it with the correct endpoint:**
```javascript
const data = await callApi("/JS/voucher/all-requests-summary", null, "GET");

```
*(Note: Make sure your Spring Boot Controller actually maps this URL to your getAllRequestsSummary() method!)*
### 2. Add the Missing useEffect to Call the API
Right now, you have defined the fetchMyBatches function, but **you are never actually calling it** when the page loads! Because your loading state starts as true, it just spins forever waiting for a function that never runs.
**FIND your fetchMyBatches function declaration:**
```javascript
  const fetchMyBatches = useCallback(async () => {
      // ... your fetch logic
  }, [callApi, showSnackBar]);

```
**IMMEDIATELY BELOW IT, ADD this useEffect block:**
```javascript
  // --- NEW: Actually trigger the fetch when the component mounts ---
  useEffect(() => {
    fetchMyBatches();
  }, [fetchMyBatches]);

```
Once you add that useEffect and fix the URL string, the screen will instantly fetch the data on load, the loading spinner will disappear, and your beautiful new table will populate!
